solution:
CREATE TABLE students (
  student_id int GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
  email      text NOT NULL UNIQUE,
  full_name  text NOT NULL
);

CREATE TABLE courses (
  code     text PRIMARY KEY,
  title    text NOT NULL,
  capacity int  NOT NULL DEFAULT 30 CHECK (capacity > 0)
);

CREATE TABLE enrolments (
  student_id  int  NOT NULL REFERENCES students (student_id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
  course_code text NOT NULL REFERENCES courses (code) ON DELETE RESTRICT,
  grade       int  CHECK (grade BETWEEN 0 AND 100),
  enrolled_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
  PRIMARY KEY (student_id, course_code)
);

Each rule maps to one declaration. `GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY` gives a surrogate key and rejects hand-picked ids; email is a natural candidate that can change, so it is UNIQUE rather than the key. The course code is short and stable, so it is the natural primary key and enrolments can store it directly. The junction table's composite primary key both identifies the row and makes a repeated enrolment a `unique_violation`, and because primary key columns are implicitly NOT NULL a half-filled pair is rejected too. The two foreign keys carry the business decisions: CASCADE because enrolments mean nothing without the student, RESTRICT because a course with history must not vanish (the default NO ACTION would also pass here). `CHECK (grade BETWEEN 0 AND 100)` rejects 101 and -1 and lets NULL through, because a CHECK fails only on FALSE. `timestamptz` with `DEFAULT now()` records the instant unambiguously. In an interview, add: the PK index on `(student_id, course_code)` serves lookups by student, but finding the students of a course, and the RESTRICT check on a course delete, want an index on `enrolments (course_code)`.

